WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:05.960
Movies are a visual medium, and therefore,
most movies' major characters are, well, visible

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:06.760
to the audience.

00:00:07.109 --> 00:00:11.559
But as with any rule, there are notable exceptions
— and to prove it, these are just a few

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:14.420
memorable examples of film characters you
never see.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:20.080
Thanks to the success of the original Charlie's
Angels television series, which ran from 1976

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:26.730
to 1981, a film reboot was inevitable; in
2000, director McG's adaptation, which starred

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:30.200
Cameron Diaz, Drew Barrymore, and Lucy Liu,
hit theaters.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:35.380
As the titular team working out of an exclusive
agency in Los Angeles, the trio lend the film

00:00:35.381 --> 00:00:39.240
the feel of the original television series
while setting it in a more modern era and

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.440
stretching one mystery into a longer story.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:46.990
As in the original series, Charlie is perpetually
unseen, accessible only through speaker and

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:51.460
identified only as the Angels' millionaire
boss who finds them different jobs, gives

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.360
them instructions, and works through his assistant
Bosley.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:58.320
Though the film never addresses why Charlie
isn't present, aside from indicating that

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:03.090
he's frequently targeted by villains and assassins,
the closing scene shows a man quietly observing

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:07.440
the angels, who could be Charlie himself though
he still remains obscured to the viewer.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:14.540
How will we ever know you really, truly exist
unless you come down here and have a coconut

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:15.040
with us?"

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:15.680
"Faith, angels.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:17.380
It's called faith."

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:23.560
Spike Jonze is known for his inventive, ambitious
projects, and 2013's Her, which won Best Original

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:28.420
Screenplay at the 86th Academy Awards as well
as scoring a nomination for Best Picture tackles

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:32.080
the concept of artificial intelligence from
an entirely new perspective.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:36.300
Set just slightly ahead of the present day,
the film imagines a time when humans are able

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.340
to create virtual assistants using artificial
intelligence.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:44.800
Theodore played by Joaquin Phoenix feels disconnected
from the people around him after a rough divorce…

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.180
until he meets Samantha.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.440
"I can understand how the limited perspective
of an un-artificial mind would perceive it

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:50.940
that way.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:52.320
You'll get used to it."

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:59.280
As Theodore gets closer to his AI assistant
Samantha seductively voiced by Scarlett Johansson

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:03.260
he realizes that their relationship isn't
as special as he previously thought.

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:07.490
She admits that she's in similar "relationships"
and in love with hundreds of other people

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:10.960
before telling him that she and the other
AIs are leaving the human sphere.

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:15.420
As society creeps closer to the advent of
new forms of artificial intelligence, the

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:19.880
unseen assistants of Her become even more
relevant, warning audiences about the benefits

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:22.700
and costs of technology that may soon become
a reality.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:29.860
Adapted from Ira Levin's novel, Roman Polanski's
horror classic Rosemary's Baby stars Mia Farrow

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:34.450
as Rosemary, a woman who believes a Satanic
cult wants to use her baby for evil purposes.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:38.420
When Rosemary and her husband Guy move into
their new apartment in New York City, they

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:42.280
befriend an odd elderly couple who welcome
the young pair with open arms.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:46.990
However, throughout the film, Rosemary becomes
suspicious of them, and as she experiences

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:52.180
a truly horrific pregnancy, she realizes that
her child is not her husband's, but actually

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:56.470
the son of the Devil, and she must raise the
child as her own to appease this dangerous

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:56.960
cult.

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:58.660
"Read what they do, Guy!

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:03.600
They use blood in their rituals, and the blood
that has the most power is baby's blood."

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:08.599
During the film's finale, Rosemary discovers
that her child is the son of Satan, who "chose"

00:03:08.599 --> 00:03:12.599
her to carry his baby, and rather than show
the baby directly, there's simply a flash

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:15.900
of a demonic face perhaps the child's father,
Satan.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:18.900
"What have you done to its eyes?"

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:20.960
"He has his father's eyes."

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:28.480
In refusing to show an actual shot of the
child, the film leaves its appearance up to

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:33.220
the imagination, making it even more frightening
than, say, a potentially unrealistic prosthetic

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:34.220
"demon" baby.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:39.580
The second film in the long-running Ernest
film series, Ernest Saves Christmas features

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:44.500
Jim Varney's character Ernest and serves as
the holiday-themed installment in the franchise.

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:48.580
Ernest, who appeared in several commercials
for brands like Coca-Cola and and Chex, made

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:53.020
his mark by always delivering an impassioned,
off-topic monologue to his friend Vern while

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:57.000
speaking directly to the camera, which always
culminated in him proclaiming the virtues

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.280
of whatever product he happened to be pitching.

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:04.020
"Got 'im, Vern.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:05.600
Another Pacific Pilsner.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:06.640
That's a keeper!"

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:11.900
In Ernest Saves Christmas , Ernest, a taxi
driver working in Florida, inadvertently picks

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:16.080
up Santa Claus and accidentally gains possession
of Santa's magic sack of toys.

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:20.720
Through a series of misadventures, he helps
find the next man who will become Santa Claus

00:04:20.729 --> 00:04:24.599
and makes sure Christmas goes smoothly and
also throughout the film, he delivers his

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:28.680
trademark monologues to Vern, whose POV is
once again provided by the camera.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:32.839
During one notable scene, he even helps host
a Christmas party at Vern's house, much to

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:34.139
Vern's apparent chagrin.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:35.719
"Ho, ho, ho, Vern!

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:36.320
Merr—"

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:38.740
"I thought you said this Vern was a friend
of yours."

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:42.160
"D'oh, Vern's just like that: He'll do anything
for a laugh."

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:48.500
OK, so chances are you already know the whole
story of Romeo and Juliet —

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:54.540
"From forth the fatal loins of these two foes,
a pair of star-crossed lovers take their life."

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:59.580
but, in case you've been living under a rock,
here goes: Romeo and Juliet, respective heirs

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:03.780
to the warring Montague and Capulet families,
meet by chance at a party and immediately

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:08.119
fall in love, even though they're conditioned
to hate one other and even though Juliet is

00:05:08.119 --> 00:05:09.539
already engaged to someone else.

00:05:09.539 --> 00:05:13.389
Though they do their best to make a new life
together, their plans are foiled at every

00:05:13.389 --> 00:05:17.830
turn, and they eventually do the whole poison/dagger
thing, and yada yada yada.

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:19.260
You know how this one turns out.

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:20.820
"Poison!

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:22.520
I'll kiss thy lips.

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:26.280
Haply some poison doth yet hang on them."

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:32.000
Aw, crap wrong movie.

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:32.760
Hold tight.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:44.259
The play has seen plenty of adaptations over
the years, but the most popular among modern

00:05:44.259 --> 00:05:49.379
viewers is Baz Luhrman's fantastical Romeo
+ Juliet, which modernizes the sets and costumes

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:51.340
while retaining the original prose.

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:55.640
It's pretty weird, and it shouldn't work — but,
honestly, somehow it does.

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:58.000
They have made worms' meat of me.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.380
A plague on both your houses!"

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:09.240
Though most know the narrative outline, it's
easy to forget that Juliet isn't always the

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:14.050
only girl on Romeo's mind in fact, when the
story begins, Romeo is heartsick over another

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:17.689
girl, Rosaline, who seemingly won't give him
the time of day.

00:06:17.689 --> 00:06:21.909
When his friend Mercutio encourages him to
attend Juliet's party so he can forget about

00:06:21.909 --> 00:06:26.830
Rosaline, Romeo's former love is forgotten
and, in fact, never appears in the play or

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:27.920
film at all.

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:32.240
Still, she plays a relatively important part
in the whole tragic mess: If she hadn't left

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:35.280
Romeo heartbroken, he may never have even
met Juliet!

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:40.280
2010's Winter's Bone introduced Jennifer Lawrence
to mainstream film audiences.

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:45.400
Set in the Ozarks, this low-budget drama stars
Lawrence as Ree, who is solely responsible

00:06:45.409 --> 00:06:48.999
for taking care of her mentally ill mother
as well as her two younger siblings.

00:06:48.999 --> 00:06:53.180
While she attempts to teach her siblings how
to survive, she's also tasked with finding

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:57.219
her father, who has disappeared after his
arrest for making meth, and the stakes are

00:06:57.219 --> 00:07:01.689
high if she doesn't find him in time, then
her family's house and land will be seized,

00:07:01.689 --> 00:07:03.999
leaving them with a week to find somewhere
else to live.

00:07:03.999 --> 00:07:08.520
Along the way, she runs afoul of the town's
crime boss, Milton, while everyone tells her

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:12.259
that her father is already dead, and she tries
to figure out what the real story is even

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:14.080
as bondsmen hunt him down.

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:17.960
"Some of our blood at least is the same, ain't
that supposed to mean something?

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:20.099
Isn't that what is always said?"

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:25.620
As it turns out, everyone is right her father
is, in fact, dead, as Ree discovers when the

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:29.300
Milton clan shows her his body partially submerged
in a lake.

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:33.319
Left with only his hands to identify him to
the sheriff and bondsmen, Ree's journey is

00:07:33.319 --> 00:07:38.300
over and she can be at peace, but her unseen
father's character remains vital to the story,

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:41.840
as searching for him to save her family is
what drives her throughout the entire narrative.

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:47.639
The 1964 classic Dr. Strangelove or: How I
Learned to Stop Worrying and Love the Bomb

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:50.620
sprung from the mind of legendary director
Stanley Kubrick.

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:55.300
This dark comedy focused on a Cold War era
nuclear conflict between the United States

00:07:55.300 --> 00:07:56.480
and the Soviet Union.

00:07:56.620 --> 00:08:01.600
Starring legends Peter Sellers and George
C. Scott, the film's central conflict hinges

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:06.119
upon a deranged general who orders a nuclear
strike by the United States against the Soviet

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:10.380
Union and the efforts of the United States
President and his cabinet to then try to stop

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:10.880
the strike.

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:15.439
Though this might not sound like a laugh riot,
the script and Sellers' multiple performances

00:08:15.439 --> 00:08:19.789
are so hilarious that the film has become
known as one of cinema's best comedies, earning

00:08:19.789 --> 00:08:24.779
itself a place in the National Film Registry
and the third spot on the 100 Years 100 Laughs

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:25.779
list at AFI.

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:32.180
"Gentlemen, you can't fight in here — this
is the war room!"

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:37.700
Because the film centers on a conflict between
the U.S. and Soviet Union, one might think

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:41.410
the Russian premier would be fairly central,
but thanks to the focus on the United States

00:08:41.410 --> 00:08:46.320
government's response, Premier Dmitri Kissoff
is ultimately never seen, providing most of

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:50.650
the film's push and pull without ever having
to physically appear on screen and existing

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:52.910
only through phone calls to and from the President.

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:58.880
"Uh, we're doing a documentary about the Blair
Witch."

00:08:59.140 --> 00:08:59.640
"Oh."

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:01.440
"Oh, have you heard of the Blair Witch?"

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:04.420
"Oh yeah, that's an old, old, old story."

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:09.180
One of the first forays into the modern era
of the "found footage" subgenre of horror

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:14.210
films, The Blair Witch Project was filmed
over only eight days, stranding actors in

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:18.140
the woods with handheld equipment to lend
an air of authenticity to the frightening

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:18.640
story.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:20.700
"I'm not playing head games, man.

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:24.460
If anyone's playing head games, you're playing
head games, but I'm not playing head games."

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:29.580
In fact, the viral marketing campaign was
almost too successful, including the decision

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:34.590
to keep the actors from attending the film's
premiere and listing them as deceased on IMDb.

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:38.840
The production was a real-life horror in its
own right; the actors were intentionally deprived

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:43.100
of food and pranked by the crew to elicit
"real" reactions for the final product.

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:44.860
"HEEEELP!

00:09:45.340 --> 00:09:47.200
HEEEELP!

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:49.940
PLEASE HELP US!

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:52.600
HELP US!"

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:58.190
In the low-budget/high-scares flick, a group
of amateur filmmakers Heather, Mike, and Josh

00:09:58.190 --> 00:10:00.680
document their search for the mysterious Blair
Witch.

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:04.500
Though they're initially skeptical of any
supernatural phenomena, during their time

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:07.260
in the woods, they're tormented by some unseen
force.

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:11.940
When Josh goes missing, it appears as if the
Blair Witch herself is mimicking his screams

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:13.360
to lure Heather and Mike.

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:17.860
After they find an abandoned house, Mike's
camera footage ends, and Heather finds him

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:21.020
silently standing in a corner just before
she's attacked herself.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:25.800
As the film progresses, it becomes clear why
the Blair Witch is never seen: it's simply

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:27.540
far scarier that way.

00:10:27.980 --> 00:10:33.760
Based on the 1944 Pulitzer-winning stage play
of the same name by Mary Chase, the 1950 film

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:39.320
Harvey stars James Stewart and Josephine Hull
and tells a rather unconventional tale.

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:44.750
Stewart plays Elwood P. Dowd, a cheerful,
friendly lush with one very unique characteristic:

00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:48.460
his best friend is a six-foot-tall, invisible
rabbit named Harvey.

00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:52.890
Though Elwood's family, including his sister
and niece, are exasperated and concerned by

00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:57.750
Elwood's imaginary friend, his appealingly
pleasant demeanor leads to Elwood making friends

00:10:57.750 --> 00:11:01.950
at his neighborhood bar, where the bartender
always greets Harvey and serves Elwood two

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:02.440
martinis.

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:06.920
Naturally, the absence of the film's title
character is what drives the entire story,

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:11.600
as Elwood's family, fearing for his well-being
and sanity, has him committed to an institution

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:13.150
to help potentially "cure" him.

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:18.640
However, over time, Elwood's doctor, Dr. Chumley,
begins to believe Elwood and believe in Harvey.

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:24.700
"At first, Dr. Chumley seemed a little frightened
of Harvey, but that gave way to admiration

00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:25.580
as the evening wore on."

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:32.120
In the end, Elwood's loved ones save him from
a medicine that would make him "normal" and

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:34.280
stop him from seeing his beloved bunny bestie.

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:39.360
The film was incredibly well-reviewed, leaving
a lasting lesson that imagination is not a

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:43.360
flaw, but something that can brings real joy
to anyone if they'd only believe.

